In article <Jun.4.03.13.58.1989.2050@topaz.rutgers.edu> linhart@topaz.rutgers.edu (Mike Threepoint) writes:

>We have a Samurai character class, but the rank names they ended up
>with were ranks taken from a Ninja NPC article in Dragon (Chigo,
>Bushi, Genin, Chunin, Jonin).  I mentioned my displeasure to Steve
>Pearl, who cleverly asserted that assigning Ninja ranks to Samurai
>was like assigning Indian ranks to Cowboys.
>
>It's wrong.  What's right?  I think Japanese military ranks would
>suffice, but my (admittedly unthorough) research did not provide that
>info.

Japanese military ranks appear to be translations of European ranks
introduced in the mid-nineteenth century.

There was no unified ranking system for samurai.  Different fiefs had
their own rankings schemes.  Someone in fief A might not have the same
status in fief B and indeed might not even be recognized as a samurai.
The situation was so complex and diversified that when the Meiji government
pensioned samurai in the 1870s, the government had to have hearing boards
to decide what title attached to a given individual would translate into
what rank for pension purposes.

The only universally recognized ranking scheme was income (the size of
the stipend or nominal income of a fief).  Even here there is substantial
variation among fiefs and over time.

